<title>GERMANY: Public sector budget forecast from German Wise Men.</title>
<headline>Public sector budget forecast from German Wise Men.</headline>
<dateline>BONN 1996-11-15</dateline>
<text>
<p>Following are key forecasts for Germany's total public sector budgets from the 1996/97 annual report of the government's independent Council of Economic Advisers, also known as the Five Wise Men (figures are in billions of marks unless otherwise stated):</p>
<p>					 1997	  1996	1995</p>
<p> Revenue			     1,704.25    1,647     1,628.50</p>
<p>Of which:</p>
<p>  Taxes				  846.50	827	 838.60</p>
<p>  Social welfare levies	    734	   698.50    669.6</p>
<p> Expenditure			 1,827	 1,796.50  1,751.1</p>
<p>Of which:</p>
<p>  Public consumption		 716.50	702.50    675.40</p>
<p>  Wages and salaries		 844	   830.50    807.50</p>
<p>  Interest			     138	   132	 129.60</p>
<p>  Financial transfers		 49.50	 50	  51.9</p>
<p>  Investment			    79.50	 81.50     86.70</p>
<p> Budget deficit			122.50	149.50    122.60</p>
<p>Ratios in percent:</p>
<p>  Public spending as part of GDP   49.5	 50.5	50.6</p>
<p>  Taxes, levies to GDP		 42.8	 42.9	43.6</p>
<p>  Public sector deficit to GDP	3.3	  4.2	 3.5</p>
<p>-- William Boston, Bonn Newsroom, 49 228 26097145</p>
